package prompts

func generalNarrationMixRules(ctx map[string]any) string {
	if stringValue(ctx, "narrationMixPolicy", "") != "duration-ratio-v1" {
		return ""
	}
	if stringValue(ctx, "purpose", "") != "script" {
		return ` MIX PRESERVATION: narrationRatio concerns FINAL PLAYBACK DURATION. At 0 keep original playback only; At 1 keep narration only. Otherwise retain both kinds. Do NOT narrate, quote, paraphrase or spoil an original payoff. Preserve the current step's response schema; do not return a full script or originalClips when this step requests units, chapters or originals. `
	}
	if stringValue(ctx, "repairScope", "") == "single-chapter-v1" {
		return ` LOCAL MIX REPAIR: Return only the requested narration text. Preserve surrounding originals and avoid repeating their action/payoff. No full manuscript, originalClips or markers. `
	}
	if stringValue(ctx, "repairScope", "") == "mix-continuity-v1" {
		return ` MIX CONTINUITY WRITING PASS: Write fresh narration from SOURCE around LOCKED originalClips. The earlier automatic draft is unverified and may be omitted from INPUT; do not reconstruct or preserve its prose. This pass is authorized to write every narration slot, not merely patch a few words. Never change originalClips or their IDs/ranges. mixBlocks.blocks is an ordered list of narration slots: use writingBrief.narrationSlots[index].sourceCues as that slot's factual material, write slot 0, append its nextMarker, then slot 1, and so on. Global analysis is reference, not authorization to borrow another slot's event. The previousOriginal has ALREADY played: do not retell or paraphrase its action, dialogue or result. The nextOriginal has NOT played: do not reveal its answer or describe the action it will show. Move directly to a new supported consequence after playback. Each slot's targetCharacters and total characters are the current budget derived from the locked original durations and narrationRatio; this OVERRIDES the earlier video_config word_count and target duration. Do not fill a sparse slot with the next original's action or the previous original's result; leave it short or empty but retain nextMarker. Prefer concise causal storytelling over covering every event. Use approximately the requested number of spoken characters when source information supports it, no headers, bullet points, stage directions or announcer filler. Return {"script":"narration slot 0 [[ORIGINAL:1]] narration slot 1 ...","originalClips":[unchanged input originals],"issues":[]}. Silently check every marker is at the correct plot point and each neighbouring narration adds different information. This is the existing bounded writing pass, not an editorial gate or additional model call. `
	}
	return ` NARRATION / ORIGINAL RUNNING-TIME PLAN (overrides any earlier no-quota guidance): narrationRatio is a fraction from 0 to 1 of FINAL PLAYBACK DURATION, not chapter count or word count. narrationBudget provides the requested narration milliseconds, original milliseconds and narration-only character budget. Plan the FULL SCRIPT and originalClips together before splitting. At 0, return only ordered [[ORIGINAL:id]] markers with source-audio clips; no narration, no TTS or filler lead-ins. At 1, return narration only and originalClips:[]. Between 0 and 1, both narration and original playback are required. Aim for the requested duration allocation, but preserve meaningful scenes and natural speech rather than an exact millisecond quota. Estimate narration duration from the ACTUAL drafted text (Chinese approximately 6 characters/second; adapt to language), and aim for total original duration = estimated narration duration * (1-ratio)/ratio. If the draft is shorter than the target, scale BOTH parts; do not retain a long original budget against a very short narration. Prefer several substantial complete dramatic beats, not repeated one-second intercuts. Original ranges must be in source order and disjoint, with starts from supplied boundaries and enough unreserved same-event footage left for narration.
	TRANSITIONS: At intermediate ratios, put spoken setup BEFORE each original block and brief consequence/analysis AFTER it. The lead-in raises stakes or leaves a question; the original delivers the reveal, confrontation, memorable line or climax. Do NOT narrate, quote, paraphrase or spoil the answer/action that the immediately following original will play. Afterward advance the story or analyze a NEW supported consequence rather than replaying what viewers just heard. Do not use the first post-original sentence to announce the very action/result viewers just saw (for example, after an original refusal, do not say "he refused"). A lead-in must not narrate the beginning of an action that the selected original repeats. Align the original START to the first withheld beat and the post-original text to the NEXT unseen beat. In each reason, state what reveal is withheld for original playback and what new information the following narration contributes. Avoid announcer placeholders such as "听好了，接下来", "说出了那句话", and narration describing that someone is about to speak. Before returning, silently compare each original description with both neighbouring narration blocks; remove repeated facts from narration and keep useful new causal information. This self-edit happens in the SAME drafting call, not a separate review or downstream blocking gate. Reserve original intervals: narration pictures must not reuse them. Keep commentary claims tied to the observed action stage; a setup/reaction can accompany a lead-in, but do not describe an unseen action as occurring on screen.
	FULL SCRIPT CONTRACT: script contains every narration word and exactly one [[ORIGINAL:id]] per originalClips entry in order, consecutive IDs from 1. originalClips contain id, sourceStartMs, sourceEndMs, dialogue, reason and evidenceIds. For video-events-v1, dialogue is a short DISPLAY DESCRIPTION of the source moment, NOT invented verbatim dialogue, NOT narration, and never a TTS input; cite actual video event IDs covering the full source range. Preserve source sound directly. For transcript input, dialogue must match the complete selected transcript. Chapters preserve every marker as kind:original, originalClipId, text:"", org:dialogue, evidenceIds and the exact planned range. Narration chapters retain the manuscript verbatim. Never delete original markers while splitting or matching. Single-chapter repairs change only that narration chapter and must not create a new global plan. `
}
